---
label: Sibling lists
---

Several lists of one kind under one root, with rows moving between them.
Backlogs is the reference: an inbox and any number of sprint columns, all
holding work packages. Everything from the One list tab still applies; this
tab adds only what siblings need.

<%= embed Patterns::SortableListsPreview, :sibling_lists, panels: %i[source] %>

As on the One list tab, the preview's move URL points at a path that does
not exist: every drop and every move action rolls back with an error toast.

## Give every list an id

The lists accept the same item type, so each needs an `id` to tell them
apart; that value is the `list_id` the server receives. Their own `type` is
the kind of destination the server decodes and may differ between siblings:
the Backlogs inbox declares `inbox`, every sprint column `sprint`, and both
accept `work_package`:

```html
<div data-controller="sortable-lists--list"
     data-sortable-lists--list-type-value="sprint"
     data-sortable-lists--list-accepted-type-value="work_package"
     data-sortable-lists--list-id-value="<%%= sprint.id %>"
     data-sortable-lists--list-name-value="<%%= sprint.name %>">
```

A move into another list is the ordinary request with that list's
`list_type` and `list_id`; the client sends nothing extra to relocate a row.
On the server a
change of list is a reparent plus an insert, so run both in one transaction
and lock both lists: a failed insert must leave the record where it started.

`name` is announced to screen readers when a row arrives from another list
("moved to Sprint 3"), so it must be the user-facing name.

## Keyboard path between lists

The move menu from the One list tab only reorders within the row's current
list; its four directions never change the list. A keyboard user still has
to be able to change list, so the consumer adds its own actions that send
the same `PUT` with the destination's `list_type` and `list_id` and no
`prev_id`, landing the row first. Backlogs'
`WorkPackageCardMenuComponent` shows both shapes: "Move to inbox" is a form
item submitting `list_type=inbox` directly, while "Move to sprint…" and
"Move to backlog…" open a dialog to pick the destination and then submit the
same request. The preview above has no such action, so its cross-list move
is drag-only.

## Drops on empty space

An empty list is still a drop target, because the list element accepts the
drop, not its rows. A drop on the empty space of a non-empty list lands at
the end by default; set `data-sortable-lists--list-drop-position-value="start"`
on lists that read newest-first, as the Backlogs inbox does.

## Rows that may not leave

Set `data-sortable-lists--item-confined-value="true"` on a row the server
will reorder in place but refuses to relocate. It stays fully draggable and
its own list accepts it, so reordering keeps working; every other list
refuses the drop and a release there leaves the row where it was.

That is different from a row that may not move at all. Backlogs renders a
work package the user may not sort as a plain row: no `sortable-lists--item`
controller and no item id, so it neither drags nor anchors its neighbors'
drops.

## Auto-scrolling columns

Put `sortable-lists--scrollable` on the element that actually scrolls, which
is rarely the list itself. Backlogs wraps each column of lists in one:

```html
<div data-controller="sortable-lists--scrollable"
     data-sortable-lists--scrollable-allowed-axis-value="vertical">
```

Add the scrollable outlet to the root alongside the other two, with the same
id scoping:

```html
data-sortable-lists-sortable-lists--scrollable-outlet="#backlogs_container [data-controller~='sortable-lists--scrollable']"
```

`allowedAxis` accepts `vertical` (default), `horizontal` or `all`;
`maxScrollSpeed` accepts `standard` (default) or `fast`.

## Optimistic placement

By default the endpoint streams the re-rendered list back and the morph
reconciles the row's position. A board that re-renders on every drag pays
for that with flicker, so the root can ask for an event-only answer:

```html
<div data-controller="sortable-lists"
     data-sortable-lists-optimistic-value="true"
     data-sortable-lists-move-url-template-value="<%%= move_url_template %>">
```

(plus the outlet selectors from the One list tab.)

This appends `optimistic=true` to the move URL. The endpoint may then
acknowledge with an event alone and leave the client's placement standing.
Backlogs is the only surface that opts in today.

The flag is a request, not a promise. Backlogs skips the re-render only for a
same-list move whose requested anchor is verifiably the persisted one: a
blank `prev_id` with nothing above the row, or a non-blank one that really is
the row above. A cross-list move, or an anchor that has meanwhile left the
list, reloads the frame, because the persisted order and the optimistic order
have diverged. A failed request still rolls the rows back, unless a
concurrent morph has already moved them, in which case the fresher server
state wins.

## Batch selection

Selecting several rows and moving them together is in development
([AGILE-278](https://community.openproject.org/wp/AGILE-278),
[AGILE-361](https://community.openproject.org/wp/AGILE-361)). The root's
`selection_enabled` value and the collection move endpoint it drives are not
yet a stable contract; this section documents them once they settle.
